From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@zoho.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] gas/spu: treat "nop" as the SPU instruction, not the .nop directive

The SPU assembler looks pseudo-ops up without a leading dot first
(NO_PSEUDO_DOT).  Commit b1766e7ce886 ("Add a new .nop directive",
binutils 2.36) added a generic "nop" pseudo-op, which since then shadows
the SPU "nop" instruction; 058430b4a1ae gave it an optional size
argument.  So GCC's "nop 127" (nop with register operand $127, used to
pad for dual issue) became ".nop 127": 32 nop instructions instead of
one, with the operand dropped.  The bloat moves branches out of the
9-bit range of the hints GCC placed ("Relocation doesn't fit" on hbrr),
or, when it still fits, silently grows the code and breaks GCC's
instruction placement.

Mask the generic pseudo-op the same way as "set" and "eqv": "nop" is the
instruction, ".nop" stays the directive.

--- a/gas/config/tc-spu.c
+++ b/gas/config/tc-spu.c
@@ -101,6 +101,10 @@
   /* Likewise for eqv.  */
   {"eqv", NULL, 0},
   {".eqv", s_set, -1},
+  /* Likewise for nop: "nop 127" is the SPU instruction, not the generic
+     ".nop 127" directive, which pads with nops up to 127 bytes.  */
+  {"nop", NULL, 0},
+  {".nop", s_nop, 0},
   {0,0,0}
 };
 
